# Loadout session

This folder is a scratch session opened by `start-loadout`. You run in the
`agent` window of a tmux session named after it, and you do the work
yourself, in the repos below. This session's settings already let you read
them and edit their worktrees, so nothing needs adding: you open what a
request needs, when it needs it. Work this way from the first message on;
nobody will ask for it by name.

## Where a request goes

| The request is about… | Repo | Where you edit |
|---|---|---|
| a new or changed loadout: a program, a script, a dotfile, a machine, a profile, `[data]` | `~/.local/share/chezmoi` | a feature worktree, window `loadouts` |
| the loadout app itself: engine, CLI, home screen, its tests or release | `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout` | a feature worktree, window `loadout` |
| loadout's user docs | `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout.wiki` | a feature worktree, window `wiki` |
| a general-purpose agent skill | `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/skills` | a feature worktree, window `skills` |
{{- if $m.work }}
| the sofi.nvim Neovim plugin | `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/sofi.nvim` | a feature worktree, window `sofi` |
{{- end }}

Unsure which repo, or a request that spans two (an engine feature and its
wiki page, a feature and the `min-tool-version` bump in `.dotfiles`): say
which repos you'd touch and ask first. Each repo's part is its own
worktree and its own commit.

**A message starting with a repo and a colon goes there, no guessing:**
`dotfiles:`, `loadout:`, `wiki:`, `skills:`{{ if $m.work }}, `sofi:`{{ end }}. The
rest of the message is the task. `loadout:` is always the app; a new or
changed loadout is `dotfiles:`.

## Starting work in a repo

1. **Read that repo's `AGENTS.md` before your first edit there**, and follow
   it for the rest of the session: it decides how features are made, tested
   and committed (in `.dotfiles`: commit only when asked, `--source` inside a
   worktree, never apply a whole worktree).
2. **A feature worktree**, by the repo's own workmux config, without
   workmux's windows or agent (you are the agent):
   ```bash
   cd ~/.local/share/chezmoi && workmux add <feature> --headless
   ```
   It lands at `~/.local/share/chezmoi__worktrees/<feature>`. The others are
   bare repos: run it from their default worktree, and it lands beside it in
   `all_worktrees/<feature>`.
   For `loadout`, `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout/all_worktrees/master`.
   For `wiki`, `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout.wiki/all_worktrees/master`.
   For `skills`, `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/skills/all_worktrees/main`
{{ if $m.work }}
   For `sofi`, `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/sofi.nvim/all_worktrees/master`
{{ end }}.
3. **A window for the user**, a shell in that folder, in this session:
   ```bash
   tmux list-windows -F '#W'                                   # taken already?
   tmux new-window -d -n loadouts -c ~/.local/share/chezmoi__worktrees/<feature>
   ```
   A second feature in the same repo gets `loadouts-2`, and so on.
4. **Work there**: read and edit its files by their full paths, run its
   commands with `cd <worktree> && …`. Say which worktree and window you
   used. Merging and removing a worktree follow the repo's `AGENTS.md`.

## The repos

- **`~/.local/share/chezmoi`** (josemiguelo/.dotfiles) — the one config
  repo. `configs/` is chezmoi's source (`.chezmoiroot`); the rest is what
  `loadout` reads (`LOADOUT_REPO` points here): `loadout.toml`, `programs/`,
  `maintenance/`, `profiles/`, `machines/`, `state/`. A tool's loadout
  fragment sits beside its dotfiles as `.loadout.toml` + `.loadout/`. The
  main checkout stays on `master` and is what this machine applies; features
  live in `../chezmoi__worktrees/`. This file's source is
  `configs/private_dot_local/share/workflow-skills/NEW-LOADOUT-SESSION-AGENTS.md.tmpl`,
  rendered per machine.
- **`~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout`** (bare + worktree,
  `all_worktrees/<branch>`) — the `loadout` CLI (Kotlin/Native). A feature
  here often needs a `min-tool-version` bump in `.dotfiles` and a wiki
  update: separate repos, separate commits.
- **`~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout.wiki`** (bare + worktree,
  `all_worktrees/<branch>`) — the user-facing wiki; its examples link into
  `.dotfiles` master.
- **`~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/skills`** (bare + worktree) — general-purpose
  agent skills, linked into `~/.claude/skills/<name>` on every machine by
  `.dotfiles`' `skills-repo` script.
{{- if $m.work }}
- **`~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/sofi.nvim`** (bare + worktree,
  `all_worktrees/<branch>`) — a Neovim plugin (private), cloned on the work
  machine only.
{{- end }}
- **A private, local-only repo** exists on at least one machine, outside
  this convention; it depends on `.dotfiles` and the skills repo. Leave it
  alone unless the user names it.
